Found by the k175 worker, confirmed by the dispatcher: `perl -Ilib -e 'use Skilletor::Error::Config; eval { Skilletor::Error::Config->throw("boom") }; print "$@\n"'` prints `Skilletor::Error::Config=HASH(0x...)`, not `boom`. lib/Skilletor/Error.pm declares `use overload q("") => sub { $_[0]->message }, bool => ..., fallback => 1;` followed by `use namespace::clean;`, and its POD says the object stringifies to its message. Likely namespace::clean (or Moo) loses the overload. Every caller that prints "$@" (raider, die without a handler) shows the HASH. Done when: an error object stringifies to its message (a test in t/ that fails today), the CLI output unchanged (the CLI uses ->message), conformance green.

Cause found (worktree agent-aa1f4512e9d68047e): namespace::clean, not Moo. overload.pm keeps its table as CODE entries in the package stash ('((', '()', '(""', '(bool'); namespace::clean records every CODE symbol present when it is used and deletes them at end of compile, so 'use overload' before it is wiped (a bare package without Moo shows the same; without namespace::clean, or with overload after it, it works). Fix: Error.pm uses overload after namespace::clean. New perl/t/01-error.t: 22/24 red on HEAD, green with the fix. Running full suites next.

Drift, not in scope (for the skilletor board, dispatcher to decide): npm run check-dist is a false red in a worktree whose node_modules is a symlink to the main checkout's. esbuild follows the link and names bundled modules '../../../node_modules/...' instead of 'node_modules/...'. With that prefix normalised, the fresh build is byte-identical to the committed dist/skilletor.js. Worktree-isolated workers cannot use check-dist as advertised.

